WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.029
Welcome to Week Ten of CS104.

2
00:00:03.029 --> 00:00:09.086
This lecture studies sequences, first as ordered lists of numbers and then

3
00:00:09.086 --> 00:00:12.619
through their behavior as the index grows.

4
00:00:12.619 --> 00:00:18.676
We will write sequences with explicit formulas, use summation and product notation,

5
00:00:18.676 --> 00:00:20.695
and calculate finite sums.

6
00:00:20.695 --> 00:00:26.752
In the second half, we ask whether sequence terms approach a fixed

7
00:00:26.752 --> 00:00:27.257
number.

8
00:00:27.257 --> 00:00:33.314
Graphs give intuition, while the epsilon definition tells us exactly what a

9
00:00:33.314 --> 00:00:34.324
limit means.

10
00:00:34.324 --> 00:00:40.381
We will prove several limits directly, recognize two ways a sequence can

11
00:00:40.381 --> 00:00:42.905
diverge, and apply limit laws.

12
00:00:42.905 --> 00:00:48.962
Keep track of where the index starts: changing a starting index changes

13
00:00:48.962 --> 00:00:53.000
the terms in a sum or a sequence.

14
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.720
Here is the lecture's route.

15
00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:02.247
Section eight point one represents sequences with explicit formulas, summation signs, and

16
00:01:02.247 --> 00:01:03.335
product signs.

17
00:01:03.335 --> 00:01:09.863
We will work with arithmetic and geometric patterns, a telescoping sum, and

18
00:01:09.863 --> 00:01:12.038
a change of index.

19
00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:16.934
Section eight point two turns to convergence and limits.

20
00:01:16.934 --> 00:01:23.462
We will compare convergent and divergent graphs, define closeness with an epsilon

21
00:01:23.462 --> 00:01:29.989
neighborhood, use an epsilon and threshold argument for proofs, and finish with

22
00:01:29.989 --> 00:01:31.077
limit theorems.

23
00:01:31.077 --> 00:01:37.604
The notation may look compact, but every expression is an instruction about

24
00:01:37.604 --> 00:01:42.500
which terms to include and what operation to perform.

25
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.375
We begin with ways to represent a sequence.

26
00:01:46.375 --> 00:01:52.188
A sequence is an ordered collection of terms, each identified by an

27
00:01:52.188 --> 00:01:52.672
index.

28
00:01:52.672 --> 00:01:58.484
The order matters: the second term is not interchangeable with the fifth.

29
00:01:58.484 --> 00:02:04.297
A formula can describe a term directly from its index, while a

30
00:02:04.297 --> 00:02:07.688
recurrence can describe it through earlier terms.

31
00:02:07.688 --> 00:02:13.016
Summation and product notation then combine a selected range of terms.

32
00:02:13.016 --> 00:02:18.828
In the examples ahead, we will often start indexing at zero, as

33
00:02:18.828 --> 00:02:20.281
the slide does.

34
00:02:20.281 --> 00:02:26.094
Whenever you see a formula involving n, check whether n equal to

35
00:02:26.094 --> 00:02:29.000
zero is the first permitted input.

36
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.288
These familiar lists show that sequences can have very different patterns.

37
00:02:34.288 --> 00:02:37.654
Here the natural numbers begin at zero.

38
00:02:37.654 --> 00:02:42.462
The even numbers are zero, two, four, and so on.

39
00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:48.231
The geometric example doubles from one to two, four, eight, and beyond.

40
00:02:48.231 --> 00:02:54.000
The Fibonacci list starts one, one, two, three, five, eight: after the

41
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.769
first two entries, each term is the sum of the preceding two.

42
00:02:59.769 --> 00:03:04.096
The primes start two, three, five, seven, eleven, thirteen.

43
00:03:04.096 --> 00:03:09.865
Each list is ordered, but not every list is best described by

44
00:03:09.865 --> 00:03:12.269
the same kind of rule.

45
00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:18.038
The starting value and index convention must be stated before we write

46
00:03:18.038 --> 00:03:19.000
a formula.

47
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:24.586
The slide denotes a sequence by a sub n with indices beginning

48
00:03:24.586 --> 00:03:25.517
at zero.

49
00:03:25.517 --> 00:03:29.241
An explicit formula gives a sub n directly.

50
00:03:29.241 --> 00:03:33.897
For the even numbers, a sub n equals two n.

51
00:03:33.897 --> 00:03:39.483
For the displayed geometric sequence, it equals two to the power n.

52
00:03:39.483 --> 00:03:45.069
The Fibonacci sequence also has the closed formula on the slide, involving

53
00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:50.655
the two numbers one plus or minus square root of five, divided

54
00:03:50.655 --> 00:03:51.586
by two.

55
00:03:51.586 --> 00:03:57.172
That expression is correct for the displayed one, one, two, three sequence

56
00:03:57.172 --> 00:04:02.759
when n starts at zero, but it is far less convenient than

57
00:04:02.759 --> 00:04:05.086
adding the previous two terms.

58
00:04:05.086 --> 00:04:10.672
An explicit formula is useful when it simplifies the question; it is

59
00:04:10.672 --> 00:04:13.000
not automatically the best representation.

60
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:18.047
The capital sigma means add terms while the index runs through the

61
00:04:18.047 --> 00:04:18.888
stated range.

62
00:04:18.888 --> 00:04:23.935
Sum from k equals m to n of a sub k means

63
00:04:23.935 --> 00:04:28.981
a sub m plus a sub m plus one, continuing through a

64
00:04:28.981 --> 00:04:29.822
sub n.

65
00:04:29.822 --> 00:04:31.505
The bounds are inclusive.

66
00:04:31.505 --> 00:04:36.551
In the first example, summing k itself lists m, m plus one,

67
00:04:36.551 --> 00:04:38.654
and all integers through n.

68
00:04:38.654 --> 00:04:43.701
In the second, summing two k from zero to n includes the

69
00:04:43.701 --> 00:04:47.486
first term zero and the final term two n.

70
00:04:47.486 --> 00:04:52.533
There are n plus one terms in that second sum, not n.

71
00:04:52.533 --> 00:04:57.579
Reading the lower and upper bounds before doing algebra prevents most indexing

72
00:04:57.579 --> 00:04:58.000
mistakes.

73
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.213
This exercise turns a written list into one sigma expression.

74
00:05:02.213 --> 00:05:07.269
For positive n, the numerators run from one to n plus one,

75
00:05:07.269 --> 00:05:11.060
while the denominators run from n to two n.

76
00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:13.167
Let i begin at zero.

77
00:05:13.167 --> 00:05:18.222
The first numerator is i plus one, and the first denominator is

78
00:05:18.222 --> 00:05:19.486
n plus i.

79
00:05:19.486 --> 00:05:24.542
When i reaches n, the term becomes n plus one over two

80
00:05:24.542 --> 00:05:27.069
n, exactly the last term shown.

81
00:05:27.069 --> 00:05:32.125
Therefore the entire list is the sum, for i from zero through

82
00:05:32.125 --> 00:05:36.338
n, of i plus one divided by n plus i.

83
00:05:36.338 --> 00:05:41.394
We chose the index by matching both endpoints, not merely by recognizing

84
00:05:41.394 --> 00:05:43.500
a pattern in the middle.

85
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.653
Now expand a sigma expression.

86
00:05:45.653 --> 00:05:50.819
The summand is negative one to the power i, divided by i

87
00:05:50.819 --> 00:05:55.125
plus one, and i runs from zero to two n.

88
00:05:55.125 --> 00:05:58.139
At zero the term is positive one.

89
00:05:58.139 --> 00:06:03.306
At one it is negative one half, at two positive one third,

90
00:06:03.306 --> 00:06:05.889
and the signs continue to alternate.

91
00:06:05.889 --> 00:06:11.056
The upper index two n is even, so the final term is

92
00:06:11.056 --> 00:06:14.069
positive one over two n plus one.

93
00:06:14.069 --> 00:06:19.236
Writing the first, a representative middle, and the last term is a

94
00:06:19.236 --> 00:06:24.403
quick way to check both the denominator shift and the final sign.

95
00:06:24.403 --> 00:06:29.569
The sigma sign is compact; it never changes which terms are actually

96
00:06:29.569 --> 00:06:30.000
included.

97
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.179
The sum from k equals one to n of one over k

98
00:06:35.179 --> 00:06:38.632
times k plus one has a useful decomposition.

99
00:06:38.632 --> 00:06:43.812
One over k times k plus one equals one over k minus

100
00:06:43.812 --> 00:06:45.970
one over k plus one.

101
00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:51.150
After substitution, the first few terms are one minus one half, then

102
00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:55.897
one half minus one third, then one third minus one fourth.

103
00:06:55.897 --> 00:06:58.919
Every interior fraction cancels with its neighbor.

104
00:06:58.919 --> 00:07:04.098
Only one and negative one over n plus one remain, so the

105
00:07:04.098 --> 00:07:09.278
sum equals one minus one over n plus one, or n over

106
00:07:09.278 --> 00:07:10.573
n plus one.

107
00:07:10.573 --> 00:07:12.299
This is called telescoping.

108
00:07:12.299 --> 00:07:17.479
Always write enough terms to see which boundary pieces survive; cancellation is

109
00:07:17.479 --> 00:07:20.500
not an excuse to forget an endpoint.

110
00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:25.222
An arithmetic sequence has a constant difference d between neighboring terms.

111
00:07:25.222 --> 00:07:30.373
Starting at a sub zero, its n-th term is a sub zero

112
00:07:30.373 --> 00:07:32.090
plus n times d.

113
00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:37.241
For example, the exercise sequence two, five, eight has d equal to

114
00:07:37.241 --> 00:07:37.670
three.

115
00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:42.821
To sum from index zero through n, pair the first and last

116
00:07:42.821 --> 00:07:47.972
terms: each pair has value a sub zero plus a sub n.

117
00:07:47.972 --> 00:07:53.123
There are n plus one terms, so the total is the first

118
00:07:53.123 --> 00:07:57.415
plus last, multiplied by n plus one, divided by two.

119
00:07:57.415 --> 00:07:59.561
The number of terms matters.

120
00:07:59.561 --> 00:08:04.712
A formula that uses n rather than n plus one would miss

121
00:08:04.712 --> 00:08:06.000
the index-zero term.

122
00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:10.562
A geometric sequence has a constant ratio r, so its n-th term

123
00:08:10.562 --> 00:08:14.364
is a sub zero times r to the power n.

124
00:08:14.364 --> 00:08:18.926
Adding from index zero to n gives a sub zero times the

125
00:08:18.926 --> 00:08:23.107
sum of one, r, up to r to the power n.

126
00:08:23.107 --> 00:08:27.669
When r is not one, this equals a sub zero times r

127
00:08:27.669 --> 00:08:32.231
to the n plus one minus one, divided by r minus one.

128
00:08:32.231 --> 00:08:36.793
One way to derive it is to multiply the sum by r

129
00:08:36.793 --> 00:08:40.595
and subtract the original, leaving only the two boundary terms.

130
00:08:40.595 --> 00:08:44.397
The displayed fraction is undefined at r equal to one.

131
00:08:44.397 --> 00:08:48.959
In that case every term equals a sub zero, and the sum

132
00:08:48.959 --> 00:08:52.000
is n plus one times a sub zero.

133
00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.386
Apply the two sum patterns.

134
00:08:54.386 --> 00:09:00.114
In part a, two, five, eight, through three hundred two is arithmetic

135
00:09:00.114 --> 00:09:01.545
with difference three.

136
00:09:01.545 --> 00:09:07.273
The index from the first to the last advances one hundred times,

137
00:09:07.273 --> 00:09:10.614
so there are one hundred one terms.

138
00:09:10.614 --> 00:09:16.341
Their average is one hundred fifty-two, and the sum is fifteen thousand

139
00:09:16.341 --> 00:09:17.773
three hundred fifty-two.

140
00:09:17.773 --> 00:09:23.500
In part b, powers of two run from exponent ten through exponent

141
00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:25.409
twenty-nine, giving twenty terms.

142
00:09:25.409 --> 00:09:31.136
The geometric sum is two to the power thirty minus two to

143
00:09:31.136 --> 00:09:32.568
the power ten.

144
00:09:32.568 --> 00:09:38.295
You may factor out two to the power ten and sum one

145
00:09:38.295 --> 00:09:41.159
through two to the power nineteen.

146
00:09:41.159 --> 00:09:44.500
Both approaches include the endpoints exactly once.

147
00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:48.100
The capital pi means multiply rather than add.

148
00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:53.500
Product from k equals m to n of a sub k expands

149
00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:58.900
to a sub m times a sub m plus one through a

150
00:09:58.900 --> 00:09:59.800
sub n.

151
00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:04.300
The first example multiplies consecutive integers from m to n.

152
00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:09.700
The second multiplies k over k plus one from one to n.

153
00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:15.100
Writing the factors gives one half times two thirds times three fourths,

154
00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:19.600
and so on, ending at n over n plus one.

155
00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:23.650
Almost everything cancels, leaving one over n plus one.

156
00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:25.900
This is a telescoping product.

157
00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:31.300
As with sums, the cancellation pattern is easiest to trust after the

158
00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:34.000
first and last factors are explicit.

159
00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:37.357
Summation and product notation obey different combination rules.

160
00:10:37.357 --> 00:10:42.393
Two sums over the same range can be combined term by term:

161
00:10:42.393 --> 00:10:47.429
sum a sub k plus sum b sub k equals sum of

162
00:10:47.429 --> 00:10:50.366
a sub k plus b sub k.

163
00:10:50.366 --> 00:10:54.563
A constant can be moved inside or outside a sum.

164
00:10:54.563 --> 00:10:59.598
For products, product a sub k times product b sub k equals

165
00:10:59.598 --> 00:11:03.375
product of a sub k times b sub k.

166
00:11:03.375 --> 00:11:08.411
But do not transfer a sum rule to a product without checking

167
00:11:08.411 --> 00:11:08.830
it.

168
00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:13.866
For example, a constant factor outside a product would be multiplied once

169
00:11:13.866 --> 00:11:15.125
for each index.

170
00:11:15.125 --> 00:11:20.161
The common range from m through n is crucial in all three

171
00:11:20.161 --> 00:11:21.000
displayed identities.

172
00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:25.881
The exercise sets a sub k equal to k plus one and

173
00:11:25.881 --> 00:11:29.136
b sub k equal to k minus one.

174
00:11:29.136 --> 00:11:34.017
In part a, combine the two sums over the same range before

175
00:11:34.017 --> 00:11:38.898
substituting: a sub k plus twice b sub k becomes k plus

176
00:11:38.898 --> 00:11:43.780
one plus two times k minus one, which simplifies to three k

177
00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:44.593
minus one.

178
00:11:44.593 --> 00:11:49.475
So the result is one sum of three k minus one from

179
00:11:49.475 --> 00:11:50.695
m through n.

180
00:11:50.695 --> 00:11:54.356
In part b, combine the products term by term.

181
00:11:54.356 --> 00:11:59.237
The new factor is k plus one times k minus one, or

182
00:11:59.237 --> 00:12:03.712
k squared minus one, giving one product over the same bounds.

183
00:12:03.712 --> 00:12:08.593
Notice that a plus sign between products would not permit this multiplication

184
00:12:08.593 --> 00:12:09.000
rule.

185
00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:13.811
A change of index renames the running variable and changes the bounds

186
00:12:13.811 --> 00:12:14.613
with it.

187
00:12:14.613 --> 00:12:19.423
The slide starts with the sum from k equals one through n

188
00:12:19.423 --> 00:12:21.829
of one over k plus one.

189
00:12:21.829 --> 00:12:24.635
Set j equal to k plus one.

190
00:12:24.635 --> 00:12:29.446
When k equals one, j equals two; when k equals n, j

191
00:12:29.446 --> 00:12:31.050
equals n plus one.

192
00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:33.455
The summand becomes one over j.

193
00:12:33.455 --> 00:12:38.266
So the same sum is from j equals two through n plus

194
00:12:38.266 --> 00:12:40.270
one of one over j.

195
00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:45.081
No term changed: both versions list one half, one third, through one

196
00:12:45.081 --> 00:12:46.685
over n plus one.

197
00:12:46.685 --> 00:12:51.495
A common error is to change the symbol in the summand but

198
00:12:51.495 --> 00:12:53.500
leave the old bounds behind.

199
00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:59.242
We now shift from finite combinations of terms to the long-run behavior

200
00:12:59.242 --> 00:13:00.677
of a sequence.

201
00:13:00.677 --> 00:13:06.419
Section eight point two asks what happens to x sub n when

202
00:13:06.419 --> 00:13:08.333
n grows without bound.

203
00:13:08.333 --> 00:13:13.597
A limit is not simply the next term in a pattern.

204
00:13:13.597 --> 00:13:19.339
It is a number that the terms eventually approach as closely as

205
00:13:19.339 --> 00:13:20.296
we demand.

206
00:13:20.296 --> 00:13:26.038
Some sequences grow away without bound, and others oscillate forever, so they

207
00:13:26.038 --> 00:13:27.952
have no finite limit.

208
00:13:27.952 --> 00:13:33.694
We will first read these possibilities from graphs, then replace the visual

209
00:13:33.694 --> 00:13:38.000
intuition with a precise definition that can support proofs.

210
00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:43.703
The plotted points represent x sub n equal to one over n,

211
00:13:43.703 --> 00:13:46.079
starting at n equals one.

212
00:13:46.079 --> 00:13:51.782
The values one, one half, one third, and so on remain positive,

213
00:13:51.782 --> 00:13:53.683
but they become smaller.

214
00:13:53.683 --> 00:13:56.535
The dots approach the horizontal axis.

215
00:13:56.535 --> 00:14:02.238
We say the sequence converges to zero and write that its limit

216
00:14:02.238 --> 00:14:05.564
as n goes to infinity is zero.

217
00:14:05.564 --> 00:14:10.317
Convergence does not require a term to equal the limit.

218
00:14:10.317 --> 00:14:13.644
None of these positive terms is zero.

219
00:14:13.644 --> 00:14:19.347
Instead, for every requested positive distance from zero, sufficiently late terms fall

220
00:14:19.347 --> 00:14:20.772
within that distance.

221
00:14:20.772 --> 00:14:26.000
The graph suggests the answer; an epsilon proof will establish it.

222
00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:29.465
These two graphs diverge for different reasons.

223
00:14:29.465 --> 00:14:35.404
On the left, x sub n equals n plus one, so the

224
00:14:35.404 --> 00:14:38.869
points grow beyond every fixed finite value.

225
00:14:38.869 --> 00:14:44.808
On the right, x sub n equals negative one raised to n

226
00:14:44.808 --> 00:14:45.798
plus one.

227
00:14:45.798 --> 00:14:50.747
Its terms alternate between positive one and negative one forever.

228
00:14:50.747 --> 00:14:56.687
The second sequence stays bounded, yet it still has no single limit

229
00:14:56.687 --> 00:14:59.162
because neither alternating branch disappears.

230
00:14:59.162 --> 00:15:05.101
Thus bounded does not automatically mean convergent, and divergent does not always

231
00:15:05.101 --> 00:15:06.091
mean unbounded.

232
00:15:06.091 --> 00:15:12.030
When examining a proposed limit, look at what every sufficiently late term

233
00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:15.000
does, not just a few dots.

234
00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.448
Now compare two convergent patterns.

235
00:15:17.448 --> 00:15:23.323
The sequence n over n plus one equals one minus one over

236
00:15:23.323 --> 00:15:27.729
n plus one, so it approaches one from below.

237
00:15:27.729 --> 00:15:33.604
The sequence one plus negative one half to the power n alternates

238
00:15:33.604 --> 00:15:39.479
around one, but the size of the correction shrinks geometrically to zero.

239
00:15:39.479 --> 00:15:41.438
It also approaches one.

240
00:15:41.438 --> 00:15:45.844
Oscillation itself is not the problem; persistent oscillation is.

241
00:15:45.844 --> 00:15:51.719
The graph on the right crosses above and below the target, yet

242
00:15:51.719 --> 00:15:54.656
its points settle closer and closer.

243
00:15:54.656 --> 00:16:00.531
This distinction will help when we test whether a sequence meets the

244
00:16:00.531 --> 00:16:02.000
formal limit definition.

245
00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:07.520
To define closeness, choose a real number a and a positive epsilon.

246
00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:13.040
The epsilon neighborhood of a contains all real x for which the

247
00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:17.640
absolute distance between x and a is less than epsilon.

248
00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:23.160
On the number line this is the open interval from a minus

249
00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:25.460
epsilon to a plus epsilon.

250
00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:30.060
Epsilon can be very small, but it must be positive.

251
00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:33.280
The strict inequality excludes the two endpoints.

252
00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:38.800
This neighborhood language lets us turn the phrase close to a into

253
00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:42.940
a specific condition that we can verify with algebra.

254
00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:48.000
The next slide applies it to the tail of a sequence.

255
00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:53.291
A sequence x sub n converges to x if, for every positive

256
00:16:53.291 --> 00:16:58.582
epsilon, we can find an index K, possibly depending on epsilon, such

257
00:16:58.582 --> 00:17:03.873
that every term with n at least K lies in the epsilon

258
00:17:03.873 --> 00:17:05.195
neighborhood of x.

259
00:17:05.195 --> 00:17:10.486
Equivalently, the absolute value of x sub n minus x is less

260
00:17:10.486 --> 00:17:14.014
than epsilon for all n at least K.

261
00:17:14.014 --> 00:17:19.305
In the picture, a few early crosses lie outside the horizontal band,

262
00:17:19.305 --> 00:17:24.595
but after the vertical line at K, all crosses stay inside it.

263
00:17:24.595 --> 00:17:29.886
The order of the words matters: epsilon is chosen first, then we

264
00:17:29.886 --> 00:17:31.209
may choose K.

265
00:17:31.209 --> 00:17:36.500
K must control the whole tail, not just one selected late term.

266
00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:39.682
Use the definition for two basic examples.

267
00:17:39.682 --> 00:17:45.136
A constant sequence x sub n equals c has distance zero from

268
00:17:45.136 --> 00:17:50.591
c at every index, so any valid threshold K works for every

269
00:17:50.591 --> 00:17:51.500
positive epsilon.

270
00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:56.955
For x sub n equal to one over n, choose an integer

271
00:17:56.955 --> 00:17:59.682
K larger than one over epsilon.

272
00:17:59.682 --> 00:18:05.136
Then whenever n is at least K, the distance from zero is

273
00:18:05.136 --> 00:18:10.591
one over n, at most one over K, and therefore less than

274
00:18:10.591 --> 00:18:11.045
epsilon.

275
00:18:11.045 --> 00:18:13.318
This gives the threshold explicitly.

276
00:18:13.318 --> 00:18:18.773
The examples also show why a proof begins with an arbitrary epsilon

277
00:18:18.773 --> 00:18:21.500
rather than selecting one convenient tolerance.

278
00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:27.442
The proposed limit of three n plus one over two n plus

279
00:18:27.442 --> 00:18:29.422
five is three halves.

280
00:18:29.422 --> 00:18:32.888
Subtract the proposed limit before choosing K.

281
00:18:32.888 --> 00:18:38.830
A common denominator gives absolute difference thirteen over four n plus ten.

282
00:18:38.830 --> 00:18:44.277
This is less than thirteen over four n for positive n.

283
00:18:44.277 --> 00:18:50.218
Given any epsilon above zero, choose an integer K larger than thirteen

284
00:18:50.218 --> 00:18:51.704
over four epsilon.

285
00:18:51.704 --> 00:18:57.646
Then all n at least K make the difference less than epsilon.

286
00:18:57.646 --> 00:19:02.597
The computation identifies a concrete threshold and proves the limit.

287
00:19:02.597 --> 00:19:08.539
Dividing numerator and denominator by n also suggests three halves, but the

288
00:19:08.539 --> 00:19:12.500
epsilon argument explains why the approach is rigorous.

289
00:19:12.500 --> 00:19:18.038
The exercise asks us to prove four to the n over n

290
00:19:18.038 --> 00:19:21.269
factorial tends to zero using the definition.

291
00:19:21.269 --> 00:19:25.423
Its neighboring-term ratio is four over n plus one.

292
00:19:25.423 --> 00:19:30.962
Once n is at least seven, that ratio is at most one

293
00:19:30.962 --> 00:19:31.423
half.

294
00:19:31.423 --> 00:19:36.962
Hence every later term is at most the seventh term times one

295
00:19:36.962 --> 00:19:40.654
half raised to the number of additional steps.

296
00:19:40.654 --> 00:19:44.808
A geometric tail with ratio one half approaches zero.

297
00:19:44.808 --> 00:19:50.346
For any positive epsilon, choose K large enough that this upper bound

298
00:19:50.346 --> 00:19:55.885
is below epsilon; then every n at least K satisfies the required

299
00:19:55.885 --> 00:19:56.346
inequality.

300
00:19:56.346 --> 00:20:01.885
The factorial eventually wins because its new factor n plus one keeps

301
00:20:01.885 --> 00:20:06.500
increasing while the numerator gains only a factor of four.

302
00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:11.778
Here x sub n equals one plus negative one to the power

303
00:20:11.778 --> 00:20:12.218
n.

304
00:20:12.218 --> 00:20:17.495
Even indices give two, while odd indices give zero, and both kinds

305
00:20:17.495 --> 00:20:19.694
of indices occur arbitrarily late.

306
00:20:19.694 --> 00:20:22.333
Suppose there were a limit L.

307
00:20:22.333 --> 00:20:24.972
Choose epsilon equal to one half.

308
00:20:24.972 --> 00:20:30.250
Beyond the promised threshold, an even term would have to lie within

309
00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:35.528
one half of L, and an odd term would also have to

310
00:20:35.528 --> 00:20:38.167
lie within one half of L.

311
00:20:38.167 --> 00:20:43.444
But two and zero are distance two apart, too far for both

312
00:20:43.444 --> 00:20:47.843
to fit within those half-unit neighborhoods of the same L.

313
00:20:47.843 --> 00:20:50.042
So no such limit exists.

314
00:20:50.042 --> 00:20:54.000
This is a direct contradiction to the epsilon definition.

315
00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:59.879
Once two sequences have limits x and y, the limit theorems let

316
00:20:59.879 --> 00:21:01.348
us combine them.

317
00:21:01.348 --> 00:21:07.227
Sums and differences approach x plus y and x minus y; products

318
00:21:07.227 --> 00:21:13.106
approach x times y; and a fixed positive integer power approaches the

319
00:21:13.106 --> 00:21:15.066
corresponding power of x.

320
00:21:15.066 --> 00:21:20.944
For a quotient, the denominator terms must be nonzero and the denominator

321
00:21:20.944 --> 00:21:23.884
limit y must also be nonzero.

322
00:21:23.884 --> 00:21:29.763
That last condition is essential: dividing by values tending to zero may

323
00:21:29.763 --> 00:21:31.232
behave very differently.

324
00:21:31.232 --> 00:21:36.131
These rules are efficient after the component limits are known.

325
00:21:36.131 --> 00:21:42.010
They do not prove convergence of a sequence whose pieces have no

326
00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:42.500
limits.

327
00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:44.690
Apply the limit laws carefully.

328
00:21:44.690 --> 00:21:49.947
For a real constant c, c over n to the power k

329
00:21:49.947 --> 00:21:53.889
tends to zero when k is a positive integer.

330
00:21:53.889 --> 00:21:59.146
The slide says k belongs to the natural numbers, but this lecture

331
00:21:59.146 --> 00:22:01.774
included zero among natural numbers earlier.

332
00:22:01.774 --> 00:22:07.031
At k equal to zero, the expression equals c and generally does

333
00:22:07.031 --> 00:22:10.973
not approach zero, so the positive-exponent condition is necessary.

334
00:22:10.973 --> 00:22:16.230
For part b, divide the cubic fraction by n cubed: two plus

335
00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:21.487
one over n, divided by one plus five over n cubed, tends

336
00:22:21.487 --> 00:22:22.363
to two.

337
00:22:22.363 --> 00:22:27.619
In part c, n over one plus two n tends to one

338
00:22:27.619 --> 00:22:32.000
half, and its fifth power tends to one over thirty-two.

339
00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:38.278
The final slide marks a continuation of the limit-theorem discussion, without adding

340
00:22:38.278 --> 00:22:40.370
a new displayed formula.

341
00:22:40.370 --> 00:22:44.556
Before leaving the lecture, connect the two halves.

342
00:22:44.556 --> 00:22:50.833
A sequence rule tells us how to identify its terms; sigma and

343
00:22:50.833 --> 00:22:57.111
pi notation combine a finite range; a limit concerns the behavior of

344
00:22:57.111 --> 00:22:59.204
all sufficiently late terms.

345
00:22:59.204 --> 00:23:05.481
For a direct proof, start with an arbitrary positive epsilon and find

346
00:23:05.481 --> 00:23:09.144
a threshold that controls the entire tail.

347
00:23:09.144 --> 00:23:15.421
For a calculation, use limit laws only after checking their conditions, especially

348
00:23:15.421 --> 00:23:21.699
the nonzero denominator in a quotient and the positive exponent in the

349
00:23:21.699 --> 00:23:22.745
power-over-n example.

350
00:23:22.745 --> 00:23:28.500
The mastery questions will ask you to make those choices yourself.
